  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nA significant pattern change is set to begin this weekend with a\nlarge Pacific trough slowly moving southeastward across the\nWestern U.S., eventually pushing the subtropical high into the\nSouthern Plains by Sunday. Our main forecast concern with this\nchange will be the increasingly favorable conditions for\nthunderstorms, especially by Sunday and Monday. The influence of\nthe trough may occur as early as late Friday night and early\nSaturday as a relatively strong jet streak moves through\nCalifornia into the Great Basin. This should result in increasing\nupper level support for showers and thunderstorms, but we will\nalso have to have enough moisture. For now, guidance is unclear on\nthe rain chances for early Saturday, but the NBM is trying to put\na 30-40% chance in after midnight. The daytime hours Saturday are\njust as unclear as moisture is likely to continue to improve, but\nwill it be enough to overcome a modest capping inversion. \nGuidance does show PoPs of 30-40% during the evening hours \nSaturday from Phoenix and areas to the north and east of Phoenix,\nbut little chance to the west. Temperatures Saturday will cool a \nbit from Friday, but there should still be enough residual heat to\npush daytime highs to around 110 degrees across the western lower\ndeserts to 107-109 degrees in the Phoenix area.\n\nSunday into possibly Monday very well could be quite active as\nguidance is pointing at very good moisture advection into Arizona,\nsomewhat directed by a passing tropical system to the southwest of\nsouthern Baja. Atmospheric parameters improve on Sunday with\nMUCAPEs anywhere from 1000-2000 J/kg and little if any CIN during\nthe afternoon hours. The presence of the trough over or just to\nthe northwest of our area should also provide some modest shear\nand upper level support for storm potential on Sunday and/or\nMonday. Depending on the evolution of the trough, heating, and \nhow much moisture gets pulled into our area, we may need to worry\nabout a strong to severe storm potential as well as a heavy\nrainfall threat as PWATs may reach near 1.75\". Drier air will also\nbe trying to work into western portions of our area by Sunday,\nleaving little chance for rain over southeast California and\npotentially southwest Arizona. NBM PoPs reflect this with at most\n10-20% chances over the western deserts, but PoPs rise to 40-60%\nfrom Phoenix and areas to the north and east of Phoenix on Sunday.\nThere is much higher model uncertainty going into the mid to\nlatter half of next week as GEFS favors moisture staying mostly in\nplace while the EPS favors some more significant drying. Part of\nthis uncertainty is due to a highly active tropic region to our\nsouthwest which very well could influence our region at times.\n\nTemperatures are at least slated to drop a good amount over the\nweekend into early next week with forecast highs falling back to\nnear normal on Sunday and then at or just below the normal range\nearly next week. We can't even rule out having highs in the 90s\nfor a day across south-central Arizona if the rain activity is\nwidespread enough on Sunday and/or Monday.",
